WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:06.890
Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:08.189
My name is Andreas Hofheinz,

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:11.090
and today I'm going to talk
about interactive multilayer

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:14.230
maps in Shiny with
the leafdown package.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.990
For my talk, I'm going to use
the Meteostat Weather's Record Keeper

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:19.720
data set, which
contains monthly measurements

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.110
from over 2000 weather
stations in the United States,

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.750
which measure variables such
as the average temperature

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.430
and precipitation.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:29.530
And the question I want
to discuss in my talk

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:31.900
is how can we most
effectively explore

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.290
this data using insights into
how to solve common problems

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:39.210
and overcome the
limitations of simple maps?

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.370
When we work with
spatial data, well, it

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.350
is usually helpful
to visualize the data

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.780
on a map because of the
geographical context

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.300
and the intuitiveness
that comes with it.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.880
In this example, we see the
average temperature per state

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.580
and already at the first
glance, it becomes clear

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.320
that the average
temperature in the South

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.450
is higher than it
is in the North.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:01.930
However, one phenomenon we often
observe when we aggregate data

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:04.150
at higher levels, such
as the state level,

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.170
is these hard breaks
at the borders.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.380
Here it looks like
everywhere in California

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.340
the average temperature
is quite high.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.910
And as soon as you take a
single step into Oregon,

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.260
the average temperature
drops significantly,

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:17.150
which is probably not the case, right?

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.690
But the question
is, how can we

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.180
explore this in more
detail or in other words,

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.630
how can we zoom in?

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.910
And one way to do
this is to drill down

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.920
to see the data at a
more detailed level,

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.080
in this case at
the County level.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.660
In this visualization, we
see that there is indeed

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.340
no hard breaks at
the state borders,

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.260
but rather smooth
transition, and

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.870
that the overall high average
temperature of California

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.660
is primarily caused
by the hot South.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.690
So you might wonder why we don't
aggregate the data directly

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:47.640
then at the County
level, and why do we even

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.970
bother to aggregate it at the
state level in the first place?

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.950
Well, let's have a
look at what happens

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.930
when we plot the average
temperature for all

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.120
the counties at once.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.640
In my opinion, this
looks quite cluttered

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.580
and the overall temperature
trend is not that easy to see,

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.280
especially when we compare it
to the state aggregation map

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.600
from before.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.840
I'd argue that it is much easier
to see on the right hand side

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.760
that the average
temperature in the South

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.950
is higher than it
is in the North.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.870
And this is one
reason why it's really

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.300
important in many
spatial data analysis

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.910
to switch between several
aggregation layers,

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.870
to be able to analyze both the
overall trends as well as more

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.580
regional trends.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.470
OK, let's return
to our map showing

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.540
the average
temperature per County.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:35.050
Often we are interested not
only in one variable at a given

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:38.230
point in time, but in multiple
variables and the relationship

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.630
or the development over time.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:42.850
So another question
is, how can we

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:48.480
visualize multiple variables
simultaneously using a map?

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.750
And one approach to do this
is to embed the map in a Shiny

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.060
dashboard and connect
it to other dashboard

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.400
elements like charts or tables.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.040
By selecting the regions
of interest in the map,

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.400
we can then display additional
variables or the development

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.950
over time in the other
dashboard elements.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.960
In this case, this
allows us to analyze

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.540
the relationship between
the average temperature

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.850
and monthly
precipitation, as well

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:14.420
as the development of the
average temperature over time.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.940
But I don't want to just
describe these solutions here.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.630
I also want to tell you that we
have already implemented them

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.930
in the leafdown package.
With just a few lines of code,

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:27.340
The leafdown
package enables you

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:31.600
to quickly create maps that are
embedded in a Shiny dashboard

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.580
and have selectable
regions, which

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.770
connects the map to
other dashboard elements,

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.590
allowing you to visualize
multiple variables at once

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:42.390
as well as the
development over time.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.030
They also have the drill down
and drill up functionality

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.520
that allows you to switch
between several aggregation

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.090
layers, such as from the State
layer to the County layer,

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:54.820
to analyze the data
in more detail.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:57.650
And these maps are not limited
to only two map layers.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:03:59.890
Instead, we can drill
all the way down

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:01.840
to the very individual
weather stations

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:05.260
and compare their measurements
in the graphs as well.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:07.300
Allowing the user to
specifically select

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:10.900
the regions to drill down for
avoids unnecessary clutter

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:15.060
and increases the
computational efficiency.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.250
So if you're now interested
in quickly creating

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.649
such flexible and
powerful maps by yourself,

00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:21.839
I can highly recommend you
have a look at our

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:22.860
leafdown package.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:25.410
On this slide, I've provided
the GitHub link for you

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.750
as well as the key
facts and features.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:31.250
Thank you very much for your
attention and have a great day.

